ted operational score:** 85/100 — LOW — APPROVE\n*This tool is for demonstration purposes only.*\n\n### Summary\nThe AI reviewer reported 1 confirmed actionable finding(s).\n\n### Findings\n- **MEDIUM · POLICY_OR_COMPLIANCE · VNet configured with unresolvable custom DNS servers contradicting documented intent**\n  - Category rationale: Policy NET-002 requires that Virtual Networks must NOT use public DNS servers and must use Azure DNS or internal DNS only. The VNet is configured with dns_servers = ['10.0.0.4', '10.0.0.5'], which are private RFC-1918 addresses and could constitute internal resolvers. However, these addresses are not verified to be reachable or operational within the VNet (no DNS resolver, VM, or forwarder resource is provisioned in TC01), and the README states 'dns_servers = []' is the intentional selection of Azure-provided DNS — directly contradicting the deployed configuration. The configured custom DNS servers point to addresses with no backing resource in this deployment, which means DNS resolution may silently fail for any workload or private endpoint that relies on it, including the AMPLS private DNS zones linked to this VNet.\n  - Evidence: azurerm_virtual_network.vnet in terraform/network.tf: dns_servers = [\"10.0.0.4\", \"10.0.0.5\"]. README.md: 'dns_servers = []' explicitly selects Azure-provided DNS. Five azurerm_private_dns_zone_virtual_network_link resources in nsg.tf link the private DNS zones to this VNet, relying on DNS resolution for AMPLS private endpoint connectivity.\n  - Analysis: azurerm_virtual_network.vnet sets dns_servers = [\"10.0.0.4\", \"10.0.0.5\"]. The README explicitly states that 'dns_servers = []' intentionally selects Azure-provided DNS. No DNS resolver, forwarder, VM, or NVA at those addresses is provisioned in TC01. The five private DNS zones (privatelink.monitor.azure.com, privatelink.oms.opinsights.azure.com, etc.) are linked to this VNet and rely on correct DNS resolution for the private endpoint to function. Custom DNS servers that are unreachable or non-existent will prevent NXDOMAIN-correct resolution of the private link FQDNs, breaking the intended private-only ingestion and query path for the Log Analytics workspace. The inline comment states 'Custom resolver; it must be reachable from this VNet' — acknowledging the dependency — but no such resolver is deployed.\n  - Impact: If the custom DNS servers at 10.0.0.4 and 10.0.0.5 are unreachable (no backing resource deployed), DNS queries from within the VNet — including resolution of the five privatelink zones — will fail or time out. This breaks the private endpoint path to the Log Analytics workspace and AMPLS, potentially causing diagnostic log ingestion to fail or fall back to public endpoints. It also contradicts the documented secure posture described in the README.\n  - Recommendation: If Azure-provided DNS is the intended resolver as documented in the README, set dns_servers = [] (or omit the attribute) in azurerm_virtual_network.vnet. If custom DNS forwarding is genuinely required, provision a DNS resolver or forwarder resource at the declared addresses within this VNet and ensure it forwards privatelink zones correctly to Azure DNS (168.63.129.16).\n\n### Policy mappings\n- **NET-002** — Virtual Networks must NOT use public DNS servers. Use Azure DNS or internal DNS only. (finding F-001)\n\n### Recommendation\nReview and remediate the confirmed findings above.\n","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/abdulwaheed384/ai-pr-review/issues/comments/6082203414/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":{"id":15368,"client_id":"Iv1.05c79e9ad1f6bdfa","slug":"github-actions","node_id":"MDM6QXBwMTUzNjg=","owner":{"login":"github","id":9919,"node_id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjk5MTk=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9919?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users